'databases_helpblurb' => "Databases allow you to create collections of data records for your site.  You can use databases to store reviews, recipes, links, or anything else you want.<br /><br />
		First, you will need to create the actual database.  After you have done this, you will need to enter into the &quot;Manage Fields&quot; page for the database you created and define the fields to collect within the database.  Common fields include a title field, and a body field (e.g. the actual review, or the recipe instructions).  Be sure to flag which field represents the title field for the database when you create it, and be sure to do the 
		same with the content field.  There are many different field types with various options, designed to allow you to collect the pieces of information you need for your database.<br /><br />
		After you have configured your database and fields, you can optionally create categories in and assign moderators to the database.  Neither of these things are necessary, but they are available to you if you wish to make use of the features.<br /><br />
		Once you have configured your database, fields, and categories (if you wish to use them), you can begin adding records to the database.  This can be done from the ACP here, or you can do it from the front end of the website if appropriate permissions have been set.<br /><br />
		Finally, you will need to embed the database into a page in order for it to be available to your users.  While you can create databases that you only utilize and manage from the ACP, to allow the page to be available to your users you will need to create or edit a page and embed the appropriate parse tag.  Use the template tag help window when you add the page to insert the correct parse tag to embed the database into the page.",
